# Black Knight Controller - Architecture Refactor
## Project: Return to the Modern PowerCockpit Vision

Background
----------

Black Knight Controller has evolved into an orchestration platform, but the current implementation is still somewhat deployment-centric.

This refactor intentionally steps back and re-establishes the architecture around the original PowerCockpit philosophy using modern cloud-native technologies.

The primary object is no longer a VM, container, or Kubernetes deployment.

The fundamental object is a **Node**.

A Node represents any manageable enterprise object.

Examples include:

- Bare metal servers
- Virtual machines
- Containers
- Kubernetes Nodes
- Kubernetes Pods
- Services
- Databases
- Network devices
- Storage
- Applications
- Pipelines
- Git repositories

Everything is a Node.

Nodes belong to one or more Groups.

Groups belong to one or more Clusters.

Pipelines operate on Groups and Nodes.

The objective is to create a living enterprise knowledge graph rather than simply provisioning infrastructure.


Phase 1 - Node Model
--------------------

Create a generic Node model.

Each Node should support:

- Unique ID
- Name
- Node Type
- Tags
- Labels
- Desired Role
- Current State
- Desired State
- Facts
- Relationships
- Group Memberships
- Cluster Memberships
- Business Ownership
- Security Classification
- Lifecycle State

Node Types should initially include:

- VM
- BareMetal
- Container
- KubernetesNode
- KubernetesPod
- Database
- Service
- Switch
- Router
- Storage
- Pipeline
- Repository


Phase 2 - Provisioning
----------------------

Revisit provisioning.

Provisioning should become procedural.

Support:

- DHCP management
- PXE / iPXE
- Network boot
- Netboot image selection
- VM creation
- Bare metal provisioning
- SSH bootstrap

Provisioning is no longer "create VM."

Provisioning creates a Node.

The Node then progresses through lifecycle stages.


Phase 3 - Discovery
-------------------

Immediately after provisioning:

Collect host intelligence.

Primary mechanisms:

- SSH
- Facter (--json)
- Optional Black Knight native probe
- Future PuppetDB connector
- Future Ansible facts

Normalize all discovered information into the Node model.

Facts are continuously refreshed.

Facts should become event-driven where practical rather than periodic snapshots.


Phase 4 - Knowledge Graph
-------------------------

The XED Control Database becomes the enterprise knowledge graph.

Relationships include:

Node
→ Group

Node
→ Cluster

Node
→ Depends On

Node
→ Runs On

Node
→ Hosts

Node
→ Uses

Node
→ Connects To

Node
→ Observed By

Node
→ Deploys

Node
→ Owned By

Node
→ Supports Business Function

Everything should be queryable.


Phase 5 - Visualization
------------------------

Automatically generate Mermaid diagrams.

Examples:

- Group diagrams
- Cluster diagrams
- Deployment diagrams
- Dependency diagrams
- Service flow diagrams
- Pipeline diagrams

These diagrams should be generated directly from the knowledge graph.

No manually maintained diagrams.


Example:

Group: RX Demo

rx-ui
 ↓
rx-api
 ↓
RabbitMQ
 ↓
SQL Server

Telemetry

OpenTelemetry
 ↓
Tempo
 ↓
Grafana


Phase 6 - Procedural Automation
-------------------------------

Instead of static workflows:

Create Procedures.

Examples:

Provision Kubernetes Worker

Patch Linux Host

Rotate Certificate

Join Cluster

Capture Legacy Server

Generate Container Deployment

Validate Application

Every Procedure consumes Nodes.

Every Procedure updates Nodes.

Every Procedure updates the knowledge graph.


Phase 7 - Migration Intelligence
--------------------------------

Instead of treating an existing machine as an image:

Treat it as a knowledge source.

Collect:

- Packages
- Services
- Ports
- Running Processes
- Configurations
- Dependencies
- Storage
- Certificates
- Environment Variables

Generate:

- Dockerfile
- Compose
- Kubernetes YAML
- Helm Values
- ConfigMaps
- Secrets
- Mermaid Diagrams
- Operational Documentation

The objective is not image cloning.

The objective is understanding.


Guiding Philosophy
------------------

Black Knight is not:

- a CMDB
- a Puppet replacement
- an Ansible replacement
- an observability platform
- a deployment engine

Black Knight is an Enterprise Infrastructure Intelligence Platform.

Provisioning, deployment, discovery, observability, migration, documentation, AI reasoning, and visualization are all different views of the same continuously evolving enterprise knowledge graph.

Every feature should enrich that graph.

Every visualization should be derived from that graph.

Every automation should update that graph.

The graph is the product.